ion Image $b_1\\neq b_2$ is necessary and it was long known that there exist sets of full upper Banach density not containing infinite sumsets Image $\\{b_1+b_2: b_1,b_2 \\in B\\}$ up to shifts (e.g., [Reference Kra, Moreira, Richter and Robertson13, Example 2.3]).\nFor completeness, we recall that for a set Image $A\\subset {\\mathbb N}$ its upper Banach density, denoted by Image $\\mathop {}\\!\\mathrm {d}^{*}(A)$ , is defined as the limit\n\nImage $$ \\begin{align*}\\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}^{*}(A)=\\limsup_{N-M \\to \\infty} \\frac{|A\\cap \\{M,M+1,\\dots,N-1\\}|}{N-M}.\\end{align*} $$\n\nMore recently, the same set of authors proposed a conjecture (see [Reference Kra, Moreira, Richter and Robertson13, Conjecture 3.10]) which generalises Theorem 1.1. Our first main result verifies this conjecture and is the following.\n\nTheorem 1.2. For any Image $A\\subset {\\mathbb N}$ with positive upper Banach density and Image $\\ell ,m \\in {\\mathbb N}$ , there exists some infinite set Image $B\\subset {\\mathbb N}$ and a number Image $t\\in {\\mathbb N}$ such that\n\nFor the purposes of contextualising our next main results, we redirect our attention to Theorem 1.1. In particular, we point out that the restriction Image $b_1\\neq b_2$ is necessary and it was long known that there exist sets of full upper Banach density not containing infinite sumsets Image $\\{b_1+b_2: b_1,b_2 \\in B\\}$ up to shifts (e.g., [Reference Kra, Moreira, Richter and Robertson13, Example 2.3]). A natural question then is whether one can guarantee such unrestricted sumsets in sets which are large through stronger notions of density. Recall that for a set Image $A\\subset {\\mathbb N}$ , its asymptotic upper and lower densities, denoted by Image $\\overline {\\mathop {}\\!\\mathrm {d}}(A)$ and Image $\\underline {\\mathop {}\\!\\mathrm {d}}(A)$ respectively, are defined as the limits\n\nImage $$ \\begin{align*}\\overline{\\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}}(A)=\\limsup_{N\\to \\infty} \\frac{|A \\cap \\{1,\\ldots,N\\}}{N}\\quad \\text{and}\\quad \\underline{\\mathop{}\\!\\mathrm{d}}(A)= \\liminf_{N\\to \\infty} \\frac{|A \\cap \\{1,\\ldots,N\\}|}{N}.\\end{align*} $$\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaul Erdős — Edgepedia (https://www.edgechat.ai/paul-erdos)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; ## What has changed since 2023 ... In 2024, Bryna Kra, Joel Moreira, Florian K. His B+B+t conjecture was proved by Richter and Donald Robertson, who demonstrated that any set of natural numbers having positive upper Banach density can be shifted so that it contains the restricted sumset {b1+b2 : b1, b2 ∈ B, b1 ≠ b2} for some infinite set B; he had proposed the conjecture while trying to link Hindman's theorem with Szemerédi's theorem.\n## What has changed since 2023\n\nThree of Erdős's problems have been settled or advanced recently. In 2024, Bryna Kra, Joel Moreira, Florian K. His B+B+t conjecture was proved by Richter and Donald Robertson, who demonstrated that any set of natural numbers having positive upper Banach density can be shifted so that it contains the restricted sumset {b1+b2 : b1, b2 ∈ B, b1 ≠ b2} for some infinite set B; he had proposed the conjecture while trying to link Hindman's theorem with Szemerédi's theorem.^{18} In the dense regime, the Erdős–Sós conjecture, asserting that for every γ there is an n0 such that for all n ≥ n0 and k ≥ γn, every n-vertex graph with more than (k−2)n/2 edges contains every k-vertex tree, was proved in a 2026 arXiv paper; as a corollary, this resolved a 51-year-old problem of Erdős and Graham concerning the multicolor Ramsey numbers of trees.^{19} And \"Erdős' last equation\", formulated in a letter to Richard Guy dated 19 August 1996, one month before his death, remains a subject of active number-theory research in 2025.^{20}\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIs Sgr A* Magnetic?  Robertso